  "illustration_prompt": "Create a single publication-quality landscape 3:2 narrative illustration for an adult illustrated English-reading booklet. Finished standalone artwork, not a book mockup, not a collage of multiple unrelated panels. Sophisticated contemporary editorial gouache and watercolor, carefully controlled colored-pencil accents, velvety matte paint, subtle warm paper texture, soft natural daylight. Warm ivory paper dominates; subdued eucalyptus and sage greens structure the composition; restrained buttery lemon-yellow and dusty terracotta accents. Calm, comfortable, refined, credible drawing and natural anatomy. An airy coherent composition with one clear story focal point and at most two supporting details, generous breathing room at all edges and softly feathered painted edges into ivory. Detailed but uncluttered. Historically and scientifically appropriate objects, clothing and setting. Show the particular article's pivotal event or mechanism rather than a generic portrait or generic textbook symbols. Human figures treated with dignity; no grotesque caricature, no sensational violence or visible injuries. For disasters, emphasize the setting, evidence or human response without graphic suffering. For uncertain scientific claims, depict the observations and investigation without presenting debated mechanisms as proven. Absolutely no typography, captions, letters, numbers, logos, watermarks, diagram labels, fake writing or invented charts anywhere. Do not render invisible forces as literal magic. Avoid corporate vector icons, slick 3D rendering and childish cartoon style. High-quality, print-ready visual storytelling, matching a warm ivory-and-sage adult watercolor booklet.\n\nARTICLE-SPECIFIC SCENE: Mary Godwin, later Mary Shelley, aged eighteen in summer 1816, seated in the Villa Diodati near Geneva at a plain wooden writing table. She wears a historically plausible modest Regency day dress, and pauses thoughtfully with a quill above completely unlettered ivory paper. A rain-streaked large window shows a subdued gray-green Lake Geneva and wet garden, expressing days of confinement by the unusually cold rainy summer. One closed unlettered anthology is the only table accessory. Focus on rain feeding literary imagination through a young writer, not a generic author portrait. Do NOT show a volcano through the Geneva window: Tambora is far away in Indonesia. Do NOT show the later Hollywood bolt-neck green monster, lightning experiments, ghosts or literal magical forces. Warm ivory, sage window garden, subtle terracotta tabletop, airy mature editorial watercolor.",
